---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Analog RF Receiver">

  <section class="prose max-w-none">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">Analog RF Receiver</h1>

    <!-- Project Description -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Project Description</h2>

    <p>
      This project is a planned analog RF receiver capable of receiving both AM and
      FM radio broadcasts. The goal is to build as much of the signal path as
      possible using discrete analog circuitry, although integrated circuits may be
      introduced where necessary as the design is developed and tested.
    </p>

    <p>
      Signals received by the antenna will be routed through separate AM and FM
      signal paths. In each path, the selected radio-frequency signal will be
      filtered, amplified, and mixed with a local oscillator to convert it to a
      lower intermediate frequency. The intermediate-frequency signal can then be
      filtered and amplified before being demodulated and sent through the final
      audio stages to a speaker.
    </p>

    <p>
      In addition to receiving and playing radio broadcasts, the receiver is
      planned to include a digital visualization system. After demodulation and
      signal conditioning, an ADC will sample the signal so that a microcontroller
      can analyze its frequency content and display information about the received
      channel on an LCD.
    </p>

    <!-- Block Diagram -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Block Diagram</h2>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/rfBlock.png"
        alt="Block diagram of the AM and FM RF receiver"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      The block diagram shows the current planned architecture of the receiver.
      After entering through the antenna and protection circuitry, the incoming
      signal is directed by a band switch into either the AM or FM path. Each path
      begins with a band-pass filter and low-noise amplifier before the selected
      station is mixed with a local oscillator. This converts the desired RF signal
      to an intermediate frequency that can be filtered and amplified more easily.
    </p>

    <p>
      The two paths use different demodulation stages. The AM path uses an envelope
      detector to recover the transmitted audio signal, while the FM path uses an
      FM demodulator. Automatic gain control is included before demodulation to help
      maintain a more consistent signal level. The recovered audio then passes
      through volume control and an audio amplifier before reaching the speaker.
    </p>

    <p>
      A parallel digital path is also planned for signal analysis. The conditioned
      signal is passed through an anti-aliasing filter and sampled by an ADC before
      being processed by a microcontroller. The current processing plan includes
      DC-offset removal, windowing, a Fast Fourier Transform, and normalization.
      This would allow the LCD to display the frequency content of the currently
      selected channel, with the possibility of displaying stored waveforms or a
      spectrogram as the project develops.
    </p>

    <p>
      The design also includes its own power-management system, consisting of a
      USB-C input, battery, charging and protection circuitry, a power switch, and
      regulated supply rails for the analog and digital sections.
    </p>

    <!-- Up Next -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Next Steps</h2>

    <p>
      The next stage of the project will be to move from the system-level block
      diagram to individual circuit designs. Each section of the receiver will be
      designed and simulated separately, beginning with the RF filtering,
      amplification, mixing, and intermediate-frequency stages. The AM and FM
      demodulation circuits will then be developed and evaluated before the full
      analog signal chain is combined.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once the receiver path is established, the power system and digital analysis
      section will be developed. This will include selecting an ADC and
      microcontroller, determining an appropriate sampling rate, and implementing
      the signal-processing steps required to calculate and display the frequency
      spectrum on the LCD.
    </p>

    <p>
      Because this is currently an early-stage design, part of the development
      process will also involve determining which sections can realistically be
      implemented using discrete analog circuitry and where integrated circuits may
      provide better performance or practicality. After the individual subsystems
      have been simulated and tested, the final design can be integrated into a
      PCB and enclosure.
    </p>

  </section>

</BaseLayout>
